== Appendix A: Glossary
#strong[ADR (American Depositary Receipt)] — a US-listed certificate representing shares of a foreign company; Indian companies with US listings produce overnight ADR signals for the Indian market the next morning.
#strong[ATM/ITM/OTM] — at-the-money (strike near spot), in-the-money (strike already favourable), out-of-the-money (strike unfavourable).
#strong[Bank Nifty] — index of India\'s largest banks; the most-traded index after Nifty for options.
#strong[Block deal] — a single large trade (typically ≥₹10 crore or 500+ shares) executed off the order book between two parties.
#strong[Breakeven (options)] — the underlying level at which a position stops losing: strike plus (for call buys) or minus (for put buys) the premium paid; for sold options, the same points mark where losses begin.
#strong[Calendar spread] — selling an option in one expiry and buying the same-strike option in a farther expiry; profits from time-decay differentials.
#strong[Call option] — right (not obligation) to buy at the strike; the seller of a call has the obligation, for premium.
#strong[Cash market] — the equity market for immediate delivery, as opposed to derivatives.
#strong[Collateral] — pledged securities/FDs/cash that fund derivatives margin without cash outlay; the margin system\'s building block.
#strong[Covered call] — selling a call against stock held; caps upside, earns premium.
#strong[Crash / correction / bear market] — working definitions used in this book: a correction is roughly a 10% fall from highs; a bear market roughly 20%; a crash is a violent, multi-percent single-session or multi-session event.
#strong[DII] — domestic institutional investor (mutual funds, insurers, domestic institutions).
#strong[Doji] — a candlestick with open and close nearly equal, signalling indecision.
#strong[Drawdown] — peak-to-trough decline of capital or index.
#strong[Expiry] — the date an option contract settles. Indian index options moved from weekly expiries per index to a rationalized single-weekly-per-exchange system after 2024–25 regulation.
#strong[F&O] — futures and options.
#strong[FII] — foreign institutional investor (also FPI, foreign portfolio investor).
#strong[Firefighting (adjustment)] — restructuring a losing position (rolling, hedging, ratioing) to reduce further damage rather than exiting outright.
#strong[Gap up/down] — opening above/below the prior close, usually on overnight news.
#strong[GIFT Nifty] — the Nifty futures contract traded at GIFT City (successor to SGX Nifty, which served the same role before July 2023); trades nearly 21 hours and indicates India\'s opening direction.
#strong[Greeks] — sensitivity measures: Delta (price move per index point), Gamma (rate of change of Delta), Theta (time decay per day), Vega (sensitivity to implied volatility), Rho (rate sensitivity).
#strong[Implied volatility (IV)] — the volatility embedded in option prices; IV Rank/Percentile compare current IV to its own history.
#strong[India VIX] — the volatility index computed from Nifty options; readings below \~15 are low-volatility regimes, above \~30 signal panic.
#strong[ITM put calendar] — selling a near-month in-the-money put and buying the same strike in a farther month; a margin-efficient, directionally-biased income structure.
#strong[Lot size] — the fixed number of units per derivative contract; Nifty\'s lot moved from 75 to larger values in the 2024–25 regulatory tightening.
#strong[Margin (SPAN + exposure)] — the collateral required to carry derivatives positions; SPAN is the portfolio risk calculation, exposure an additional buffer; peak-margin rules require intraday sufficiency.
#strong[Mark-to-market (MTM)] — daily settlement of futures gains/losses.
#strong[Max open interest (max OI) strike] — the strike with the largest open interest; treated by traders as a crowd-consensus support/resistance zone to be respected or faded.
#strong[MSCI rebalancing] — periodic index-weight changes that force passive funds to buy/sell constituents mechanically.
#strong[Open interest (OI)] — total outstanding derivative contracts; rising OI with rising prices signals new longs, etc.
#strong[Option chain] — the table of all strikes with premiums, OI, and volumes for an underlying.
#strong[Payoff] — the profit diagram of a position at expiry.
#strong[PCR (put-call ratio)] — volume or OI of puts divided by calls; a sentiment gauge best read with positioning context.
#strong[Physical settlement] — post-2019 Indian rule requiring delivery of shares for in-the-money stock options at expiry.
#strong[Premium] — the option\'s price, paid by buyer, received by seller.
#strong[Put option] — right to sell at strike.
#strong[Qualified institutional placement (QIP)] — a fast private share issuance to institutions.
#strong[Ratio spread] — buying and selling unequal numbers of options (e.g., buy 1, sell 2), creating a credit or low-cost directional structure with a capped or naked tail.
#strong[Read-through trade] — trading Indian stocks on overnight signals from related foreign-listed securities (ADR moves, foreign peers\' results).
#strong[Rolling (a position)] — closing a nearing-expiry option and opening a farther one, often at a different strike.
#strong[Short covering] — buying back borrowed/sold positions, producing sharp upside moves.
#strong[Short straddle/strangle] — selling both a call and put (same strike for straddle, different for strangle); profits from range-bound decay, suffers from big moves.
#strong[Short covering rally] — a rapid rally driven by shorts closing, not new buying.
#strong[STT (Securities Transaction Tax)] — the turnover tax on trades; raised repeatedly, most sharply on options.
#strong[Stop-loss] — a pre-set exit order; this book argues mechanical stop-losses are often inferior to position sizing plus adjustment for option sellers, and shows both sides.
#strong[Straddle/strangle buyer] — one who buys both call and put to bet on a big move either way.
#strong[Synthetic future] — a long call plus short put at the same strike, replicating futures exposure.
#strong[Theta decay] — the acceleration of option time-value loss near expiry.
#strong[Tug of war] — the recurring FII-selling vs DII/retail-buying dynamic that defined Indian markets 2021–2025.
#strong[V-shaped recovery] — a sharp fall followed by an equally sharp rebound, as in 2020.
#strong[Volatility crush] — the collapse of IV after an event (budget, results) passes, hurting option buyers.
#strong[Whipsaw] — a false breakout that reverses, trapping both sides.

