For an active options seller turning over ₹10 crore in premium (notional), the annual STT bill at the 2024 rate is approximately ₹1 lakh. At the 2026 rate, it rises to ₹2.5 lakh — before brokerage, exchange fees, or GST. A trader whose net profit is ₹5 lakh on that turnover faces an effective taxation rate (transaction costs only, not income tax) of 50% of gross profit.

== Cryptocurrency: The 30% Regime
The Finance Act 2022 introduced a dedicated tax regime for virtual digital assets (VDAs) — India\'s legal classification for cryptocurrencies and NFTs. The treatment is deliberately punitive: a flat #strong[30% tax on gains] from any VDA transfer, with #strong[no deduction for expenses] (other than the cost of acquisition), no basic-exemption threshold, and #strong[no loss set-off] — losses from one VDA transaction cannot be offset against gains from another, let alone against other income. Additionally, a #strong[1% Tax Deducted at Source (TDS)] applies on every transfer exceeding ₹50,000 (₹10,000 for specified persons) in a financial year.

#quote(block: true)[\"If you trade crypto daily, you will pay 365 days of TDS in a year. The government has made crypto a \'hold and pray\' asset class.\"]
The effect is to make any frequent trading in crypto structurally unprofitable. A trader who makes 100 trades in a year with an average gain of ₹1,000 per trade — ₹1 lakh gross profit — would owe ₹30,000 in income tax plus 1% TDS (₹1,000 per trade, or ₹1 lakh total) that is locked until the tax return is filed. Their net effective tax rate on gross gains: over 130%, because the lock-up of TDS exceeds the profit. The only viable strategy under this framework is long-term holding — and hoping that the 30% tax on eventual sale does not absorb too much of the gain.

== F&O Taxation: The Practical Checklist
+ #strong[Classify correctly]: confirm with your CA that your F&O activity is non-speculative business income (most cases) and not speculative business income (only same-day-squaring in equities).
+ #strong[Track turnover]: your #emph[turnover] for audit-threshold purposes is the sum of positive and negative differences across all contracts at settlement — typically 5–10× your net P&L. Most traders are shocked at their audit-class turnover.
+ #strong[Presumptive or books]: if turnover is under ₹3 crore and you are an individual/HUF, presumptive (6%/8%) wins 80% of the time for simplicity; use actual books only if your profit margin is clearly above 6%.
+ #strong[STT impact: budget for it]: at 2026 rates, allocate at least 0.05–0.1% of notional turnover for STT alone in your annual cost model.
+ #strong[Loss carry-forward]: non-speculative F&O losses can be carried forward for 8 years — file the return on time even if loss-making, or the carry-forward lapses.

== Key Takeaways
- #strong[F&O is non-speculative business income]: full expense deduction, slab-rate taxation, 8-year loss carry-forward. Do not confuse it with intraday equity (speculative).
- #strong[The ₹10 crore turnover audit threshold] is the single most consequential number in an active trader\'s annual planning — crossing it costs ₹30,000–75,000 in audit fees and creates permanent tax-audit exposure.
- #strong[STT has risen 8× in a decade] (options, 2014–2024 baseline) and is scheduled to rise 150% higher again from April 2026. This is now the largest transaction cost for a high-turnover trader.
- #strong[Crypto\'s 30%/1% structure] makes anything beyond holding economically unviable for frequent trading in India.
- #strong[Compliance over optimisation]: the risk of a tax notice on a procedural error (missed audit, wrong classification, incorrect turnover calculation) far exceeds the theoretical benefit of any aggressive tax strategy.

